{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '1,130p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\documentclass{article}\n     2\t% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n     3\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n     4\t\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n     5\t\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n     6\t\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n     7\t\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n     8\t  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n     9\t  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n    10\t  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n    11\t  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n    12\t  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n    13\t  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n    14\t  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n    15\t  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n    16\t}\n    17\t% --- end watermark ---\n    18\t\n    19\t\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n    20\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,longtable,subcaption}\n    21\t\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n    22\t\\title{Protected Reporting After Prompt Selection:\\\\Benefits and Limits}\n    23\t\n    24\t\\begin{document}\n    25\t\\maketitle\n    26\t\n    27\t\\begin{abstract}\n    28\tSelecting a prompt on benchmark questions and reporting its selection score confounds performance with favorable sampling noise. Reserving questions for reporting removes this direct reuse, but sacrifices observations and may change which prompt is selected. We examine that trade-off with a frozen prompt bank, known-truth simulations, and genuine CPU inference on MMLU-Pro. The controlled comparison holds the selected prompt fixed and changes only the observations used to report its score. Across five evaluation seeds, protected half-split reporting reduces mean squared error relative to selection-score reuse by 43--46\\% in the sixteen-prompt simulations and reduces squared discrepancy to a separate real-model audit by 54--76\\%. These benefits are not universal: full-budget reuse has lower mean error in every synthetic condition, and reporting error does not establish better selected-prompt accuracy. Real-model comparisons use finite audits, not known population accuracy. The evidence supports reporting target, sample allocation, and audit uncertainty as necessary parts of interpreting an optimized benchmark score, rather than endorsing a uniformly superior estimator.\n    29\t% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/reductions_percent]]\n    30\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    31\t\\end{abstract}\n    32\t\n    33\t\\section{Introduction}\n    34\tAn optimized benchmark score answers a different question from an independently measured score. When a system chooses among prompt configurations using a finite question set, the selected configuration benefits both from its underlying quality and from the noise that favored it on those questions. Repeating the selected score as the performance estimate leaves those contributions entangled. This matters even when the model weights are unchanged, the prompt bank is fixed, and every answer is evaluated consistently.\n    35\t\n    36\tA conventional response is to reserve questions that do not participate in selection. Yet that prescription alone does not settle how a reported score should be interpreted. Holding out questions reduces the sample available to select a prompt and the sample available to estimate its performance. A no-search configuration can therefore have lower estimation error while being a less accurate system. Conversely, a selected configuration can have a more trustworthy report without becoming more accurate. Any empirical comparison must state which prompt is being evaluated, which observations chose it, and what reference defines estimation error.\n    37\t\n    38\tThis paper asks: \\emph{when does protected reporting reduce error after prompt selection, and what does an independent finite audit establish about that reduction?} We study a frozen bank with randomized half-split selection and reporting. The primary baseline reports the selection score of the \\emph{same} selected prompt. This isolates the reporting-data contrast from changes in the selected system. Additional baselines expose the cost of protection: full-budget selection and reuse, all-question fixed and random prompts, and an alternative half split.\n    39\t\n    40\tThe empirical contribution is deliberately bounded. Known-truth simulations separate selection optimism, reporting bias, squared error, and interval coverage. Real answer generation supplies a benchmark check, while an explicit distinction between finite-audit agreement and population accuracy prevents that check from being overinterpreted. The results show substantial improvements over same-winner reuse when selecting among sixteen prompts, but also clear counterexamples to universal error reduction. The study evaluates a conventional reporting design rather than proposing a new optimizer or a new post-selection guarantee.\n    41\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    42\t\n    43\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    44\t\\centering\n    45\t\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/reporting_overview.png}\n    46\t\\caption{Selection and reporting have different data roles. The protected and reuse reports describe the same selected prompt; full-budget selection can choose another prompt. Known truth supports population-error measurements in simulation, whereas a separate real-model audit supplies a noisy finite reference. Protection is an intended partition property, not a claim that every source of benchmark dependence has been eliminated.}\n    47\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    48\t\\end{figure}\n    49\t\n    50\t\\section{Related Work}\n    51\t\\paragraph{Selection and assessment.}\n    52\tSeparating model choice from assessment is classical \\citep{Stone1974CrossValidation,Arlot2010Survey}. Optimization of a noisy selection criterion can bias the resulting performance estimate \\citep{Cawley2010Overfitting,Varma2006Bias}. Feature-selection studies provide another setting in which assessment must encompass the selection operation \\citep{Ambroise2002Selection,Guyon2003Selection}. Our prompt-selection setting is simpler than learned feature selection, but shares the distinction between a fixed estimator and an estimator chosen using the assessment data. Cross-validation itself has multiple possible targets; its uncertainty is not determined by the number of reported splits alone \\citep{Bates2021CrossValidation,Bengio2004Variance,Varoquaux2018CrossValidation}.\n    53\t\n    54\t\\paragraph{Prompts and benchmarks.}\n    55\tPrompt sensitivity motivates examining a bank rather than treating a single wording as intrinsic to a model \\citep{Sclar2024Formatting}. Multi-prompt evaluation estimates performance across prompt variants \\citep{Polo2024MultiPrompt}; budgeted best-arm identification instead concerns choosing a strong prompt \\citep{Shi2024BestArm}. We address the report attached to the chosen prompt, not either procedure's optimization efficiency. MMLU-Pro supplies the real multiple-choice setting \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro}; this study does not reproduce the benchmark paper's model comparisons.\n    56\t\n    57\t\\paragraph{Inference after selection.}\n    58\tWinner inference and post-selection inference formalize why a data-dependent target complicates conventional uncertainty statements \\citep{Andrews2018Winners,Berk2013PostSelection}. Adaptive data analysis and reliable leaderboards consider repeated feedback from shared data \\citep{Dwork2015Adaptive,Blum2015Ladder}; our frozen, noninteractive bank does not test those guarantees. Simultaneous approximations for maxima address another inferential route \\citep{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian}, which is not implemented here. The distinction between controlling an error criterion and estimating a system's accuracy also appears in multiple testing \\citep{Benjamini1995}.\n    59\t\n    60\t\\paragraph{Uncertainty and comparisons.}\n    61\tBinomial interval behavior depends on the construction and sampling regime \\citep{Brown2001Binomial}. Resampling can estimate uncertainty, but bootstrap methodology does not automatically validate an arbitrary resampling unit \\citep{Efron1979Bootstrap,DiCiccio1996Bootstrap}. Method-comparison literature emphasizes the evaluation unit and dependence structure \\citep{Dietterich1998Tests,Nadeau2003Inference,Demsar2006Comparisons}. Accordingly, we show seed-level variation without treating overlapping benchmark samples as independent datasets or presenting an unperformed significance test.\n    62\t\n    63\t\\section{Method}\n    64\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Reporting Targets}\n    65\tLet $X_{ij}\\in\\{0,1\\}$ indicate whether prompt $j$ answers question $i$ accurately. There are $n$ available questions and $K$ prompt configurations. The objective of selection is high answer accuracy, whereas the objective of reporting is low estimation error for the selected configuration. A seeded random partition creates disjoint sets $S$ and $R$, with $|S|=|R|=n/2$. Define\n    66\t\\begin{equation}\n    67\t \\widehat j=\\arg\\max_{j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,K\\}}\\overline X_{Sj},\\qquad\n    68\t \\widehat p_{\\rm reuse}=\\overline X_{S\\widehat j},\\qquad\n    69\t \\widehat p_{\\rm protect}=\\overline X_{R\\widehat j}.\n    70\t \\label{eq:estimators}\n    71\t\\end{equation}\n    72\tTies follow the fixed order of the prompt bank. Both reports in Equation~\\ref{eq:estimators} concern precisely the same configuration. A smaller error for the protected report therefore cannot be interpreted as an increase in that configuration's accuracy.\n    73\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L97]]\n    74\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L753]]\n    75\t\n    76\tIn simulation, the reference $p_j$ is the generating marginal accuracy. We measure $(\\widehat p-p_{\\widehat j})^2$, reporting bias $\\widehat p_{\\rm protect}-p_{\\widehat j}$, and selection optimism $\\widehat p_{\\rm reuse}-p_{\\widehat j}$. In the real-model setting, $p_j$ is unavailable. We instead use the observed accuracy $\\widehat a_j$ on a separate audit set, and measure $(\\widehat p-\\widehat a_{\\widehat j})^2$. We call the latter \\emph{audit-reference error}. It includes noise in the audit reference and is not a directly observed population MSE.\n    77\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L760]]\n    78\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L969]]\n    79\t\n    80\t\\subsection{Design Rationale and Compared Approaches}\n    81\tThe rationale for protection is that observations which select $\\widehat j$ should not also determine its report. Under independent, identically distributed question sampling and a bank fixed independently of the report data, reporting on fresh observations avoids the direct maximization of report noise. These assumptions explain the design; they are not themselves established by passing implementation checks. Within a finite shared question pool, disjointness alone does not supply a general population theorem.\n    82\t\n    83\tThe primary comparison changes the reporting rows in Equation~\\ref{eq:estimators}, not the selected prompt or the total question set. It is not a comparison with selecting on all questions. To expose that distinction, \\emph{full reuse} selects the highest-scoring prompt on all $n$ rows and reports that same all-row score. Its reference is the accuracy of \\emph{its own} selected prompt. The \\emph{fixed} comparator reports the all-row accuracy of the default prompt. The \\emph{random} comparator chooses a seeded prompt index and reports its all-row accuracy. Both use their own prompt's reference, not $p_{\\widehat j}$.\n    84\t\n    85\tAn \\emph{alternative half split} selects on the first half of the supplied rows and reports on the second half. This is another protected design, not an independent methodological innovation. Because it can select a different prompt, its comparison with the randomized split is not a pure reporting-data ablation. Likewise, contrasts with fixed, random, and full-reuse configurations jointly change the sample allocation or the selected system. We retain them to reveal practical trade-offs, not to attribute every difference causally to protection.\n    86\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L760]]\n    87\t\n    88\tThe reported interval is the usual 95\\% Wilson score interval evaluated on $|R|$ Bernoulli observations. Its coverage is measured against known truth only in simulation. On real questions, the same containment indicator checks whether the interval includes the audit accuracy. These are different diagnostics. Interval width is on the accuracy scale, not an uncertainty interval for the MSE comparison.\n    89\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L746]]\n    90\t\n    91\t\\subsection{Experimental Protocol}\n    92\tThe completed evaluation contains seeds $1009,2027,3037,4051,5059$. Each seed covers synthetic and real conditions with $n\\in\\{64,256\\}$ and $K\\in\\{1,16\\}$. Thus each seed contributes eight conditions; the single-prompt conditions remove selection multiplicity without changing the reporting rule. Synthetic conditions average four regimes, each with $1024$ independently generated matrices. Real conditions contain one realized selection/report/audit comparison per seed, not thousands of repeated model fits.\n    93\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    94\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L899]]\n    95\t\n    96\tAll descriptive condition means give seeds equal weight. Within a synthetic condition, regimes and replicates have equal weight. The overall summary gives the two evidence families and their question/prompt settings equal weight; it does not pool real audit questions with individual synthetic replicates. Figures show the actual seed observations, rather than confidence bars reconstructed from marginal MSE standard errors. We make no across-seed significance claim. The available summaries do not retain paired replicate-level error differences needed for such a comparison.\n    97\t\n    98\tThe studied policy is the retained baseline, not an improved configuration discovered by the search. Three alternative allocations produced no usable scored evaluations and cannot be treated as measured losses or successful ablations. The independent final evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. This records protocol qualification, not superiority, novel methodology, or complete evidence of external validity. Diagnostic records attributed to the candidate remain unverified, and absent detailed gate diagnostics remain unknown.\n    99\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study]]\n   100\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n   101\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n   102\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n   103\t\n   104\t\\subsection{Known-Truth Construction}\n   105\tThe four synthetic regimes cross equal versus graded marginal prompt accuracies with low versus high shared-outcome coupling. Equal accuracies are $p_j=0.6$. Graded accuracies are equally spaced from $0.4$ to $0.8$; for $K=1$ the implementation uses $p_1=0.4$. Let $U_0,U_1,\\ldots,U_K$ be independent uniforms for each question and let $c\\in\\{0.1,0.9\\}$. The implemented construction is\n   106\t\\begin{equation}\n   107\t V_j=\\begin{cases}U_0/c,&U_0<c,\\\\ U_j,&U_0\\geq c,\\end{cases}\n   108\t \\qquad X_j=\\mathbf{1}\\{V_j<p_j\\}.\n   109\t \\label{eq:synthetic}\n   110\t\\end{equation}\n   111\tThe shared branch changes prompt-outcome dependence while preserving marginal accuracy. We call $c$ a coupling probability, not the exact Pearson correlation for every pair of unequal-accuracy prompts. Every matrix is generated from its seed, size, prompt count, and regime; the evaluator checks the raw uniform draws against retained hashes. This construction permits population-error and coverage measurements but does not model the full dependence or difficulty structure of natural benchmark questions.\n   112\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L938]]\n   113\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes/0/solution/cases/0/details]]\n   114\t\n   115\t\\subsection{Real Inference and Implementation}\n   116\tThe real condition uses \\texttt{HuggingFaceTB/SmolLM2-135M-Instruct} on CPU in float32 with MMLU-Pro. Model and dataset revisions are fixed and recorded in Supplementary Information. The prompt bank contains sixteen frozen, short instructions asking for a choice letter. The $K=1$ condition uses only the default instruction; $K=16$ uses the full bank. A reset-seeded shuffle partitions supplied question keys, reporting weights are uniform, and tie priority follows bank order. The policy receives question identifiers and subject strata, but not answer observations; it does not adapt the bank using within-evaluation feedback.\n   117\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/runtime_manifest.json]]\n   118\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/split_manifest.json]]\n   119\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70]]\n   120\t\n   121\tFor each seed, the evaluator samples $n+512$ questions without replacement from its retained pool; the final $512$ form the audit. The benchmark subset and audit are disjoint within a condition. Samples overlap across conditions and seeds, so this design does not create independent benchmark replications. Normalized question stems and normalized question/option content are checked for split overlap. These checks do not exclude pretraining exposure or all semantic duplicates.\n   122\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L694]]\n   123\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L912]]\n   124\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   125\t\n   126\tAnswer generation applies the model's chat template and deterministically chooses one token constrained to legal answer letters. Inputs longer than $2044$ tokens and invalid responses count as errors; they are not silently omitted. Batches contain up to eight inputs, and a question-content/prompt cache reuses outputs. This is direct constrained answering, not free-form reasoning. The inference worker fixes its tensor seed and uses deterministic operations. The small model and decoding restriction are part of the evaluated configuration and limit transfer to larger or reasoning-capable systems.\n   127\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L574]]\n   128\t\n   129\tFor selected-versus-default accuracy, the evaluator resamples audit subjects with replacement, retaining all paired question outcomes within each sampled subject. It takes percentile endpoints from $200$ bootstrap draws. These descriptive 95\\% intervals are neither simultaneous across settings nor a validation of the bootstrap's sampling properties. They address an accuracy difference, not the error of the protected report.\n   130\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L987]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
