{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '226,274p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"Evaluator-owned controls check constant outcomes, copied prompt columns, a deliberately contaminated report, effective input permutation, and reset/replay. The deliberately contaminated control records failed independence, as intended. The supplied aggregate control-failure and replay-discrepancy values are zero. Those facts support specific implementation checks, not full statistical validity. The final record marks qualification successful, while candidate diagnostic trust is explicitly unverified; missing gate-level detail is not inferred from the retained metrics. The inspected evaluator file matches its recorded digest, but differently scoped source-tree digests do not establish an undocumented stronger identity.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_failure_rate]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/replay_discrepancy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n\n\\paragraph{Resources and reproducibility.}\nThe recorded seed evaluations take approximately $31$--$51$ minutes each, totaling $3.40$ hours when their durations are summed. This is a sum of reported wall durations, not measured CPU-hours, inference-only time, or an end-to-end budget accounting. GPU use, energy, and realized token totals are not supplied. The inference implementation is CPU based and caches predictions, so multiplying conditions by question counts would overstate distinct model calls. Supplementary Information and the accompanying reproduction guide identify versions, commands, seeds, and the script producing every quantitative table and figure. No new model inference or simulation was run to write this paper.\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/episode_durations_seconds]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/duration_sum_seconds]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L574]]\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nProtected reporting answers a narrower question than whether automatic prompt optimization works: it concerns how accurately a selected system's performance is described. In the evaluated sixteen-prompt settings, a held-apart half sample yields lower mean error than reusing the same prompt's selection score. Known-truth simulations show the accompanying reduction in signed optimism, and real inference provides a finite-audit check. Neither establishes that protected reporting improves selected-prompt accuracy or dominates alternative uses of the available observations.\n\nThe negative comparisons matter equally. Full-budget reuse has lower mean error in every synthetic condition, fixed-prompt reporting can be easier to estimate, and two protected splits can differ substantially on a realized benchmark sample. Thus reliability cannot be summarized by naming an estimator without its target and observation budget. The practical implication is to disclose selection and reporting data roles, retain budget-aware comparators, and distinguish population-error evidence from audit agreement. The current evidence supports that bounded reporting discipline, not a general guarantee for adaptive optimization or deployment on new question populations.\n% @evidence[[analysis/summary.json#/condition_means]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Complete Seed-Level Results}\nTable~\\ref{tab:seed} gives every recorded condition. Error values are multiplied by $10^3$; target accuracy, coverage, and interval width remain on the unit scale. Synthetic targets and diagnostics average four regimes with $1024$ replicates each. A real row has a single audit-reference error and a binary containment indicator, so a value of one is not an estimate of certain population coverage. 