{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"jq -r '.references[] | \\\"\\\\(.citation_key // .key): \\\\(.title)\\\\n  \\\\(.consulted // .consultation // .source_url // .url // empty)\\\"' references_provenance.json; jq '.' literature/Polo2024MultiPrompt.json; sed -n '130,250p' build_artifacts.py; jq '{summary,dangling,keys:keys}' scientific-evidence/verification-report.json\"","aggregated_output":"Wang2024MMLUPro: MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.01574\nDemsar2006Comparisons: Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets\n  https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v7/demsar06a.html\nDietterich1998Tests: Approximate Statistical Tests for Comparing Supervised Classification Learning Algorithms\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1162/089976698300017197\nPolo2024MultiPrompt: Efficient multi-prompt evaluation of LLMs\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17202\nShi2024BestArm: Efficient Prompt Optimization Through the Lens of Best Arm Identification\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09723\nSclar2024Formatting: Quantifying Language Models' Sensitivity to Spurious Features in Prompt Design or: How I learned to start worrying about prompt formatting\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/2310.11324\nDwork2015Adaptive: Preserving Statistical Validity in Adaptive Data Analysis\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/1411.2664\nBlum2015Ladder: The Ladder: A Reliable Leaderboard for Machine Learning Competitions\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.04585\nChernozhukov2013Gaussian: Gaussian approximations and multiplier bootstrap for maxima of sums of high-dimensional random vectors\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/1212.6906\nAndrews2018Winners: Inference on Winners\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.3386/w25456\nBerk2013PostSelection: Valid post-selection inference\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/12-AOS1077\nBates2021CrossValidation: Cross-validation: what does it estimate and how well does it do it?\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/2104.00673\nBrown2001Binomial: Interval Estimation for a Binomial Proportion\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/ss/1009213286\nEfron1979Bootstrap: Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/aos/1176344552\nStone1974CrossValidation: Cross-Validatory Choice and Assessment of Statistical Predictions\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1974.tb00994.x\nGuyon2003Selection: An Introduction to Variable and Feature Selection\n  https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v3/guyon03a.html\nVaroquaux2018CrossValidation: Cross-validation failure: Small sample sizes lead to large error bars\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/j.neuroimage.2017.06.061\nNadeau2003Inference: Inference for the Generalization Error\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1023/A:1024068626366\nBenjamini1995: Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1995.tb02031.x\nVarma2006Bias: Bias in error estimation when using cross-validation for model selection\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1186/1471-2105-7-91\nAmbroise2002Selection: Selection bias in gene extraction on the basis of microarray gene-expression data\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1073/pnas.102102699\nArlot2010Survey: A survey of cross-validation procedures for model selection\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/09-SS054\nCawley2010Overfitting: On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation\n  https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html\nBengio2004Variance: No Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation\n  https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v5/grandvalet04a.html\nDiCiccio1996Bootstrap: Bootstrap confidence intervals\n  https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/ss/1032280214\n{\n  \"title\": \"Efficient multi-prompt evaluation of LLMs\",\n  \"authors\": [\n    \"Polo, Felipe Maia\",\n    \"Xu, Ronald\",\n    \"Weber, Lucas\",\n    \"Silva, Mírian\",\n    \"Bhardwaj, Onkar\",\n    \"Choshen, Leshem\",\n    \"de Oliveira, Allysson Flavio Melo\",\n    \"Sun, Yuekai\",\n    \"Yurochkin, Mikhail\"\n  ],\n  \"year\": \"2024\",\n  \"abstract\": \"Abstract:Most popular benchmarks for comparing LLMs rely on a limited set of prompt templates, which may not fully capture the LLMs' abilities and can affect the reproducibility of results on leaderboards. Many recent works empirically verify prompt sensitivity and advocate for changes in LLM evaluation. In this paper, we consider the problem of estimating the performance distribution across many prompt variants instead of finding a single prompt to evaluate with. We introduce PromptEval, a method for estimating performance across a large set of prompts borrowing strength across prompts and examples to produce accurate estimates under practical evaluation budgets. The resulting distribution can be used to obtain performance quantiles to construct various robust performance metrics (e.g., top 95% quantile or median). We prove that PromptEval consistently estimates the performance distribution and demonstrate its efficacy empirically on three prominent LLM benchmarks: MMLU, BIG-bench Hard, and LMentry; for example, PromptEval can accurately estimate performance quantiles across 100 prompt templates on MMLU with a budget equivalent to two single-prompt evaluations. Moreover, we show how PromptEval can be useful in LLM-as-a-judge and best prompt identification applications.\",\n  \"url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17202\"\n}\n        observations = [value for value in audit if value['case'] == f'R{size}K16']\n        for row, value in enumerate(observations):\n            axis.plot(np.array(value['interval']) * 100, [row, row], color=COLORS[0], linewidth=1.5)\n            axis.scatter(value['difference'] * 100, row, color=COLORS[0], s=20, zorder=3)\n        axis.axvline(0, color='0.5', linestyle='--', linewidth=0.8)\n        axis.set_title(f'n={size}, K=16')\n        axis.set_yticks(range(len(observations)), [str(value['seed']) for value in observations])\n        axis.set_xlabel('Selected − fixed (pp)')\n        axis.set_xlim(-4.5, 9.5)\n        axis.set_xticks([-4, 0, 4, 8])\n        axis.grid(axis='x', alpha=0.2)\n    axes[0].set_ylabel('Seed')\n    axes[0].invert_yaxis()\n    figure.tight_layout(pad=0.5)\n    save_figure(figure, 'real_audit_accuracy_difference')\n    concept = Path('figures/reporting_overview.png')\n    prompt_log = Path('figures/reporting_overview.prompts.json')\n    if not Path('.concept_figure_complete').exists() and not concept.exists():\n        figure = plt.figure(figsize=(8, 2.3))\n        figure.text(0.5, 0.5, 'Selection and reporting overview', ha='center', va='center', fontsize=14)\n        figure.savefig(concept, dpi=300, facecolor='white')\n        plt.close(figure)\n    if not prompt_log.exists():\n        write_json(prompt_log, {})\n    entries = [manifest_entry('reporting_error_comparison', 'composite', 4, 2, [2]*4, [3]*4),\n               manifest_entry('real_audit_accuracy_difference', 'composite', 2, 2, [5]*2, [1]*2)]\n    overview = manifest_entry('reporting_overview', 'diagram', 1, 1, [6], [3])\n    overview.pop('path_pdf')\n    overview.update(generator='generate-concept-figure', prompt_log_path=str(prompt_log),\n                    max_label_chars=38, estimated_min_rendered_font_pt=9)\n    write_json('figure_manifest.json', {'figures': [overview] + entries})\n    content = ['% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]',\n               r'\\begin{tabular}{llrrrrrr}\\toprule',\n               r'Setting & $(n,K)$ & Protected & Reuse & Full & Half & Fixed & Random\\\\\\midrule']\n    for label in labels:\n        family, numbers = ('Synthetic' if label.startswith('S') else 'Real audit'), label[1:].split('K')\n        content.append(f'{family} & ({numbers[0]},{numbers[1]}) & ' + ' & '.join(f'{means[label][metric]*1000:.3f}' for metric in METHODS) + r'\\\\')\n    content.append(r'\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}')\n    Path('tables/main_results.tex').write_text('\\n'.join(content) + '\\n')\n    provenance('tables/main_results.tex', 'table')\n    content = ['% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]',\n               r'\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\\toprule',\n               r'Truth & Coupling & Bias (pp) & Optimism (pp) & Coverage & Width\\\\\\midrule']\n    for regime in range(4):\n        values = [regimes[str(regime)][label] for label in ['S64K16', 'S256K16']]\n        content.append(f'{\"Equal\" if regime < 2 else \"Graded\"} & {\"Low\" if regime % 2 == 0 else \"High\"} & ' +\n                       ' & '.join(f'{np.mean([value[metric] for value in values])*scale:.3f}' for metric, scale in\n                                  [('report_bias', 100), ('signed_optimism', 100), ('coverage', 1), ('interval_width', 1)]) + r'\\\\')\n    content.append(r'\\bottomrule\\end{tabular}')\n    Path('tables/synthetic_regimes.tex').write_text('\\n'.join(content) + '\\n')\n    provenance('tables/synthetic_regimes.tex', 'table')\n    content = ['% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]',\n               r'\\begin{longtable}{rrlrrrrrr}\\toprule',\n               r'Seed & $n$ & Setting & $K$ & Report & Reuse & Target & Cov. & Width\\\\\\midrule\\endhead']\n    for case in cases:\n        label, metrics = case['case'], case['metrics']\n        size, arms = label[1:].split('K')\n        content.append(f'{case[\"seed\"]} & {size} & {\"Synth.\" if label.startswith(\"S\") else \"Real\"} & {arms} & ' +\n                       ' & '.join(f'{metrics[metric]*scale:.4f}' for metric, scale in [('report_mse', 1000), ('reuse_mse', 1000), ('selected_accuracy', 1), ('coverage', 1), ('interval_width', 1)]) + r'\\\\')\n    content.append(r'\\bottomrule\\end{longtable}')\n    Path('tables/seed_results.tex').write_text('\\n'.join(content) + '\\n')\n    provenance('tables/seed_results.tex', 'table')\n    print(json.dumps(summary['condition_means'], indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n{\n  \"summary\": {\n    \"anchored_references\": 103,\n    \"dangling_references\": 0,\n    \"manifests_checked\": 14,\n    \"resolved_references\": 75,\n    \"total_references\": 178\n  },\n  \"dangling\": null,\n  \"keys\": [\n    \"results\",\n    \"root_dir\",\n    \"schema_name\",\n    \"summary\",\n    \"verified_at\"\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
